/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  diafilter01_nf_desalting / system/controlDict

  Constant-volume diafiltration: solvent is made up as fast as it
  permeates, so the vessel volume is held and the poorly-rejected solute
  washes out while the well-rejected one stays.

  The clock is chosen from the process, not from the numerics.  ONE
  DIAVOLUME is V0 / Q_p seconds of permeation, and the run's own start-up
  line gives the numbers it is computed from:

      J_w(0) = 233 LMH = 6.47e-5 m/s   over  A = 5 m2
      Q_p(0) = 3.24e-4 m3/s            V0 = 1.0036e-2 m3
      one diavolume ~ 31 s

  so endTime 160 s is ~5 diavolumes -- where the monovalent solute is
  essentially gone and the two rejections have had room to part.  (The
  first draft of this case ran 12000 s, which is 388 diavolumes: the
  divalent solute was half washed out too and the lesson was buried
  under an operation nobody would run.)

  deltaT is the adaptive driver's STARTING step; the unit exposes a clean
  ODE form, so the stiff sweep owns the stepping from there.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoBatch;
description   "Constant-volume NF diafiltration: NaCl washed out of an MgSO4 retentate, with the OBSERVED rejection published beside the constant-R washout law it violates";

verbosity     3;

startTime       0;
endTime         160;       // s   (~5 diavolumes)
deltaT          1;         // s   (adaptive; the unit is in ODE form)
writeInterval   5;         // s
